Upstream: none found for this overflow (GitLab search 2026-11-21); open issue
sequoia-pgp/sequoia#1246 (by nwalfield, 2026-05-11) discusses turning parser assert failures into
errors.

On 32-bit targets, Subpacket::parse added serialized_len() and len for a five-octet subpacket
length without an overflow check. A length of 0xfffffffb or more wrapped the sum, so a subpacket
longer than its area passed the bounds check. Reading the subpacket body then asked the header
parser's Dup reader for cursor + len bytes, which wrapped as well, data_consume_hard returned a
short buffer, and the assert in BufferedReader::steal panicked. With overflow checks the addition
itself panics. Use checked_add and reject a sum that overflows.

A v6 signature has four-octet subpacket area lengths, and an area length of 0xffffffff is
usize::MAX on 32-bit targets. A subpacket length of 0xfffffffa then fits the area exactly, and
the body length check after the subpacket value, total_out_before + len, overflowed. Without
overflow checks it wrapped and the check failed, which rejected the subpacket. The check now
compares total_out - total_out_before with len; total_out never falls below total_out_before.

openpgp/tests/subpacket_length_overflow.rs parses v4 and v6 signatures with such lengths. On i686
both tests fail without the patch in the default test profile (overflow at the bounds check and at
the body length check), also with the rest of the series applied, and pass with it. The v6 test
also fails with only the checked_add. On x86_64 both pass either way. In i686 release builds the
v4 test fails without the patch only when fix-rawcert-body-len-overflow.patch (Dup rejects the
wrapping read) is absent as well (steal assert).

With the patch, the assert in SubpacketArea::parse that the subpacket fits the area holds whenever
Subpacket::parse returns Ok.

